# bat has no built-in Catppuccin theme; the .tmTheme files live in
# ~/.config/bat/themes/ and are compiled into bat's cache by
# scripts/22-bat-cache.sh (runs `bat cache --build`).
#
# No --theme line here on purpose. .zshrc exports $BAT_THEME from the resolved
# mode, and a --theme in this file would win over it (bat prepends config-file
# args to the command line, and --theme beats the env var). Left unset, bat
# falls back to its own --theme=auto, which sniffs the terminal background and
# picks one of the two below — so bat still themes itself correctly when it's
# invoked from somewhere that isn't an interactive zsh.
--theme-light="Catppuccin Latte"
--theme-dark="Catppuccin Mocha"
